window.docContents[147].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-IEC-80000-13":{"contents":"\n\nA catalogued kind pairs a dimensioned quantity-kind with its\nexact source in the series — the part, the printed item designation (e.g. \"13-24\"), the\nprincipal quantity symbol, and the unit symbol (for the information quantities, the special\nnamed unit — shannon, erlang, bit — rather than the bare 1). The citation travels with\nthe kind as data.\n\nRealized as structure CataloguedKind over a DimensionedKind, with cite rendering\ne.g. IEC 80000-13, Edition 2.0, 2025-02 item 13-24. The catalogue lists all 42 items in\nitem order — the traffic quantities (13-1 … 13-8), storage (13-9, 13-10), the information\ncontent and entropies (13-24 … 13-37), the rates (13-38 … 13-42), … — each paired with its\nunit symbol.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009IEC 80000-13 — Information science and technology","header":"31.1. Quantity-kinds and units of IEC 80000-13","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-IEC-80000-13"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Lesson":{"contents":"1. Pair mass. R1 quantifies over every object of pairS. Its pair mass is assembled from\nthe body masses indexed to the named pair, and a witness theorem plus an executable check\nshows 3 + 5 = 8:\n\n\n\nThe body masses remain individual quantities on their body-in-pair bearers. Only pair\nmass has the Assembles pairS massK license:\n\n\n\n\n\n2. Whole-only frequency. R2 is provable by absence: the pair sort has no assembly\nlicense for the normal-mode kind. The input and output of the boundary remain attached to\nthe pair whole; the absence rules out summing body frequencies:\n\n\n\n3. Empirical range. R3 is scoped to the nominal object P. Under the one-name-one-system\nrule, it governs every state or record of P; the predicate is defined for every P record,\nand two Bool spot checks run it against the spring's datasheet limit. The gate mentions the\nreferent, so the requirement is discharged rather than attested:\n\n\n\nThe two failed scopes are counterexamples, so the survey can pin their diagnostics without\ncounting them as valid requirements. A bare value is not a nominal object, and a name is\nnot a sort merely because it is a declaration:\n\n\n\n4. Census. The valid requirements are surveyed, every produced model port has a\ngoverning requirement, and the clean command enforces that result. The census infers a\nresult port's object domain from its member's type: R3 covers a P-only result, but leaves Q\nungoverned on a generic pair boundary. An unresolved bearer is an unchecked violation unless\nexempted. A .sort scope needs an indexed result whose object type has a Sorted instance:\nR1 and R2's results are borne by the pair whole, and the instance above maps the whole to\npairS and each part to its own sort, so the census checks their scope. An object of another\nsort is reported ungoverned. A .decided scope is checked against a deciderProof theorem\nproving its decider returns true for every result of the governed member; without that\nproof the census reports the port unchecked.\n\n\n\nAll three requirements are discharged: R1 has a theorem and a spot check, R2 has an\nabsent aggregation license, and R3 has a referent-backed gate. The invalid scopes remain\nvisible as counterexamples rather than silently entering the requirement census.\n\nThe sibling-namespace probe shows the scope gap directly: a P-scoped requirement governs\nP's result while the generic pair boundary leaves Q ungoverned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold","header":"5.7.7. Lesson","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Lesson"}});